#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166488 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166883 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d13b0d31-65b8-550f-b97b-f4ece63bf834" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/id #uuid "22a44ff4-55e7-51db-a54e-26ef23764fa4" :question/math-academy-id "e-12490" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x,y)$ and the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x,y) =\\dfrac{xy}2, \\qquad\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2y, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 4 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ Find $a$ given that $b=2.$" :question/worked-solution "According to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\frac{xy}{2}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[\\frac{{x}^{2}y}{4}]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =[\\frac{{y}^{4}}{4}]_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{2y}\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}[x]_{x=0}^{x=2y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4}2y\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[{y}^{2}]}_{y=0}^{y=4} \\\\  & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we are told that $b=2.$ So, our initial equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}64 & =f(a,2)\\cdot 16 \\\\ 64 & =\\frac{a\\cdot 2}{2}\\cdot 16 \\\\ 64 & =16a \\\\ a & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\left( 4,2 \\right) \\in D.$ Therefore, the answer is $a = 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166489 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/id #uuid "0a06a8ae-69d6-5ded-979e-149f650a1676" :question/math-academy-id "e-12491" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = xy$ on the region $D = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: -1 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: x \\leq y \\leq 3x \\big\\}$ given that the area of the region is $3$ square units." :question/worked-solution "Applying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}{\\int }_{x}^{3x}xy\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}[\\frac{x{y}^{2}}{2}]_{y=x}^{y=3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{-1}^{2}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{4}]_{x=-1}^{x=2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(16-1) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/id #uuid "13800660-08b4-50af-93b4-1013bbf41e66" :question/math-academy-id "e-12492" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y) = 15xy^2$ on the portion of the disk of radius $1$ centered at the origin, where $x\\geq 0,$ and $y \\leq 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n![](images/a3/a381fede820f9fc5ba08bb9569906d41a3e8f779de38b446c4f271ceef3a5ccd.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot \\pi \\cdot 1^2 = \\dfrac\\pi 4.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{D} f(x,y) \\ \\mathrm d A = \\iint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(r\\cos\\theta, r\\sin\\theta) \\:  r \\ \\mathrm d r \\mathrm d \\theta.\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r, \\theta),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\n\\Delta = \\left\\{ (r,\\theta) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq r \\leq 1, \\: \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2} \\leq \\theta \\leq 2\\pi\\right\\}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}15x{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}15r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot (r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}15{r}^{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{1}5{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,{[{r}^{5}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ({1}^{5}-0)\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={[{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta ]}_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =({sin}^{3}⁡(2\\pi )-{sin}^{3}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =(0-(-1)) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\nf_{\\textrm{ave}} = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi} \\cdot 1 = \\dfrac{4}{\\pi}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167134 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08263df7-a90a-5d1e-9ef0-1b115c0bcf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278664} {:answer/id #uuid "173ca95b-d1d5-55a8-a28d-2f15919a4060" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278800} {:answer/id #uuid "4c4b694d-2a3e-51c2-9eb8-1f26a851e476" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{42}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279274} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cf2e9c-9ccf-5ec1-af35-0d3039c9ab61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280196} {:answer/id #uuid "d5e09d1e-8efd-55dc-b004-c5905609fe0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08263df7-a90a-5d1e-9ef0-1b115c0bcf18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278664} :answer-field/id #uuid "b007d617-0d88-5bd3-8546-df32ba381651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b5fbae5f-d41d-5595-bcf0-ce1fbfd9706b" :question/math-academy-id "q-163063" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-4{y}^{3}$ on the region $D={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=1\\cdot 3=3.\n$$\n\nApplying the average value formula, we find that the average value of the function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{∬}_{D}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}({x}^{2}-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{x}^{2}y-{y}^{4}]_{y=0}^{y=3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(3{x}^{2}-81)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[{x}^{3}-81x]_{x=0}^{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}({1}^{3}-81\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{80}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167135 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166884 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b43eea5b-9da3-5fe0-a28c-cc01aed3627a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94baf68a-d3c4-5d97-bf9b-0ccaba1b5859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163889} {:answer/id #uuid "ab56f8ea-1480-51be-8914-aa9a0232c26b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163890} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bb76c1-b6ce-5232-906e-46ec397e0d6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163891} {:answer/id #uuid "fd36f9ef-db3d-56b4-9d88-5fed8f3ac55e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163892} {:answer/id #uuid "6734fb2e-a070-553e-813c-5f7158f56e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163893}] :answer-field/id #uuid "f854d318-2b28-51b8-8b9b-478cde98f4a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163888}] :question/id #uuid "e9cfd5e0-f192-510e-add3-93b96f5880ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-163064" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = 14xy^{2}$ on the region $D = {(x, y): 0 \\le x \\le y^{2}, 0 \\le y \\le 1}$ given that the area of the region is $\\frac{1}{3}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186167137 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2a7f429-461f-54c9-9f03-dc3edbc1acd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163901} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1ea06c-900b-518b-90c1-188a82af18c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163902} {:answer/id #uuid "4366c76e-881a-5a36-9a12-14434fead7a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163903} {:answer/id #uuid "4486d7e0-2a87-59a4-9e31-7eb1fae5055e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163904} {:answer/id #uuid "598cf0a3-fa72-52ec-a62e-8e75a30ecff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3π}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163905}] :answer-field/id #uuid "a885e1f2-0379-5509-836d-b0ab6acba537" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186163900}] :question/id #uuid "e7ccaa25-bf49-56f7-93ee-b9d797d03126" :question/math-academy-id "q-163068" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x, y) = 2x + y$ on the portion of the disk of radius $2$ centered at the origin, where $y \\ge 0$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186281653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166885 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4e5deee-e910-5bb1-9d9a-0b24886ab6c1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Average Value of a Function Over a Polar Region"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07614726-b468-5358-bc84-48ba609cfd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278660} {:answer/id #uuid "14b4450b-952d-5300-8f14-fe1403fe104c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278776} {:answer/id #uuid "5f42bacb-1dcd-5151-b6dd-29e307c8e188" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279465} {:answer/id #uuid "7821b0ff-67a0-50c1-b841-8830b1518869" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186279664} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5dd293-f356-5370-a825-276c22c64e46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186280828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07614726-b468-5358-bc84-48ba609cfd04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186278660} :answer-field/id #uuid "e23f56f9-33c4-58f2-aed2-d65d0e9b71dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9470a948-0cd8-55e7-86d6-65b250b7e800" :question/math-academy-id "q-163260" :question/problem "Find the average value of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}$ on the portion of the disk of radius $3$ centered at the origin, where $x\\le 0.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's sketch our region $D:$ \n\n![](images/25/25c1f00e588ddaa130f854d9f4e9dfe9b1158d7952a01bae8108ea4b94d3c347.png)\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\text{Area}(D)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\pi \\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe average value of the function over a region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{1}{\\text{Area}(D)}\\,{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWe will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nIn polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ={(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{3\\pi }{2}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{2}{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}+1}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{2r}{{r}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{ln}⁡({r}^{2}+1)]_{0}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡10-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2}\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10[\\theta ]_{\\pi /2}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡10(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n{f}_{\\text{ave}}=\\frac{2}{9\\pi }\\cdot \\pi \\operatorname{ln}⁡10=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡10}{9}.\n$$"}]}
